"private\" project. The Foundation Allevi, despite its recent \"private\" label for this symphony, remains a recipient of €1.2 million in public money. This isn't just a coincidence; it's the business model of modern cultural management in Italy.\n       Drafting thought: Connect the \"crowdfunding\" to the reality of the market. If 68% (from Corpo 1) or the general trend shows heavy reliance on private backing, it still masks the fact that the platform* (the Foundation) is public-subsidized.\n       Drafting thought:* Address the \"agency\" issue. The gap between the €50 ticket and the €200 resale is a symptom of a market that treats culture as a luxury commodity, facilitated by the institutional prestige of the Conservatory.\n\n       Opening:* Focus on the structural contradiction. The \"private\" nature of the symphony is a legal fiction when the entity organizing it, the Fondazione Allevi, is a frequent beneficiary of the MIC and regional coffers.\n       Middle:* Discuss the \"brand\" strategy. Allevi isn't just playing music; he's managing a portfolio. The use of the Conservatory without a tender is the pivot point. It transforms a temple of study into a premium showroom.\n       Deepening:* The \"crowdfunding\" claim serves as a shield against criticism of public spending, yet the public pays twice: once through the Foundation's subsidies and once by losing access to its most prestigious halls.\n       Closing:* A sharp, prospective evaluation.\n\n       Constraint Check:* No rhetorical questions. No \"moralizing\" (but critical). No repeating Corpo 1 (I'll avoid the specific €15k-20k quote or the \"parco divertimenti\" quote if possible, or use them as a springboard).\n       Note on the 68%: The prompt's Corpo 1 cuts off at \"68% dei finanziamenti sono arrivati\". I should probably not complete that specific sentence if I don't have the source, but I can talk about the nature of the funding. Wait, the prompt says \"VIETATO: ...inventare nomi/cifre/dichiara